OPlGhrs2VjWVHynC2dptICsXoe-2Ba20A4ghNYUoqfKtm9PUuoy898coqQgfeaIaNeBUUthVOxKIvp4kvzAudxZ-2FLJ6vbAUNENYqxuZT7EmruaRbuIRR6jgdcwUMVaAMjI528OrAg7vJ7JNZ87nM58P7OC-2B8a6O8QsMv0um2ln391ykwSdWQMn2KVYwqEMFHGCiPSV-2B0hD12vb3PJVttal-2BdL6yTZHqg5ZrChZisNlsFDLS8HOtuKK9I5LVnyvHjvggh8uWHBSt\r\n)By Ben Kesslen\r\n\r\nIs your Friday night pepperoni pizza starting to feel like a\r\nluxury expense? With the typical pie running $17, Americans are\r\nforgoing toppings to keep their costs under control. We\xe2\x80\x99re\r\ncalling it the "Plain Cheese Economy" \xe2\x80\x94 a tactical way to keep\r\nthe things you love while cutting the fluff. And it\xe2\x80\x99s a great\r\nblueprint for managing many other monthly bills.Keep&nbsp;reading (\r\nhttps://ablink.newsletter.sofi.com/ls/click?upn=u001.k-2FImUMyJvquSjfrastfytIPj6h1-2BVfWjnUDDp-2FeYQFJgaQjEiLCkaMTJKxwAZUjvx1nVvQaFmdSfPc-2FnX4SQ-2F9-2FlicEdFU0ww5A-2FVAh8JWzHz-2F4GHeHJlOzJ3S5jYRjzlDB3JbZzCEEna1b2U4SoOTHvBJPGjVk4vpr8Jw3z7M1mULQwbr1lUe2Zy4v42xDFy5SAmnkmCjAdQb2-2FFDs1b-2ByUP2xMc-2BWO5m-2FRkolwaCmrsm7ahghJQEhzhmqG8wYRcOm76WH4e-2BHX7TWnET2MAg-3D-3DX8NP_Hvirw9jLOYnqUg7ucIAtvDa4JCWhfjePQT8cfdKWfeKaKK6WyzUu0MT5hcL83ezarLAJeFzEeRP-2BR0XWfLa6c8fW4zxj7ovS1-2F9EYoTLeh3rS6R6GB21HU-2F-2BKGf2kGg3huojcJjHjIWym8yXEFQzyjdVNvPVLWptUsutYOyDtPyLKqFOZHB3DzkA72fs1ui-2BxW6IL7IFAtP-2B6M8n-2FZdFpSok7h648zdKIRXJ0jr-2FIj4Dqe5s7FPePjFi5guyqA78kERb8VyHNK1mKk6-2Fkk1c2oifBDjvkWpwE-2Fy1GIiVnCZXla4dDzCnU4NzP6gfwFVr1Dfd1ODHGHdrZ0m1lGN9dJjt7DwVysHhP8ktMd4b5R-2BLXw68cVB8J7FEDNxZns3nJave4XLg6lvpR2zyF-2BNPaZU2hCTj7SbQQtjFPO82jyNB0woDEslpCDaHTQueKHgJZfHesYFQcF-2BOrAgnS4OWH7fGQzQi-2BxFtSjNGggZYoAOSWNsOsGimzITOPlGhrs2VjWVHynC2dptICsXoe-2Ba20A4ghNYUoqfKtm9PUuoy898coqQgfeaIaNeBUUthVOxKIvp4kvzAudxZ-2FLJ6vbAUNENYqxuZT7EmruaRbuIRR6hr7WSkSn2o-2F5qn0JM6q8y1hXnTMeyk97PwsO-2FixWNIBU6v0vy-2Bt1z7yrNXDtFdAOAX-2FH3KHxD7-2F30MGrpwfSv2JJR1zhvn1Vol3yA0ntZ5ZjZtRk00tNeP1JnJpWRVAuaVVIuTj09nAuZ4l-2FhR1Otk )WEEK&nbsp;AHEAD&nbsp;ON&nbsp;WALL&nbsp;STREET\r\nBanks Kick Earnings\r\nOffwallstreet.png(\r\nhttps://ablink.newsletter.sofi.com/ls/click?upn=u001.k-2FImUMyJvquSjfrastfytIPj6h1-2BVfWjnUDDp-2FeYQFLpjhu-2F6ot-2F5dL1x7ZEqHyiUNGtzFkjjgTRgCH88hkgWT22B5jOlxdZRMhlZE7Og1Lt2UyFWoW2xKeiOaRCJS5xOWmnby9rmGoslAiDrUU9HMrSPVapevjsxlq5JOOGUrhpEqF2f0-2BgBtBuKusgNd5SIC4sJgsts-2FRnfek-2BzdW-2Br5qcf-2F90IAqB01x8mTg5HWQr12mPigAL1JH9dvSxl8mQVIiSYFIbjD-2FJIKCC-2BlVtkQ-3D-3Dnbq3_Hvirw9jLOYnqUg7ucIAtvDa4JCWhfjePQT8cfdKWfeKaKK6WyzUu0MT5hcL83ezarLAJeFzEeRP-2BR0XWfLa6c8fW4zxj7ovS1-2F9EYoTLeh3rS6R6GB21HU-2F-2BKGf2kGg3huojcJjHjIWym8yXEFQzyjdVNvPVLWptUsutYOyDtPyLKqFOZHB3DzkA72fs1ui-2BxW6IL7IFAtP-2B6M8n-2FZdFpSok7h648zdKIRXJ0jr-2FIj4Dqe5s7FPePjFi5guyqA78kERb8VyHNK1mKk6-2Fkk1c2oifBDjvkWpwE-2Fy1GIiVnCZXla4dDzCnU4NzP6gfwFVr1Dfd1ODHGHdrZ0m1lGN9dJjt7DwVysHhP8ktMd4b5R-2BLXw68cVB8J7FEDNxZns3nJave4XLg6lvpR2zyF-2BNPaZU2hCTj7SbQQtjFPO82jyNB0woDEslpCDaHTQueKHgJZfHesYFQcF-2BOrAgnS4OWH7fGQzQi-2BxFtSjNGggZYoAOSWNsOsGimzITOPlGhrs2VjWVHynC2dptICsXoe-2Ba20A4ghNYUoqfKtm9PUuoy898coqQgfeaIaNeBUUthVOxKIvp4kvzAudxZ-2FLJ6vbAUNENYqxuZT7EmruaRbuIRR6hSMvFv5-2BaWeam3vfxQDHFrR7Kxdf8o6-2Bfz0T-2BRGuJVbhRF-2B3iDmlkE3s9ZJW-2BzurJIwmuQ1ZfxWjXec8ee7Y19pY3MP54sdCapN5-2Fl1ZGH26wscG1A-2BfoOLb61fUaItf0X4G6SWrUVEiOFjYhbT0lN\r\n)By Mario Ismailanji\r\n\r\nThe unofficial start of the fourth-quarter earnings season is\r\nhere, and as is tradition, the nation\xe2\x80\x99s financial titans will be\r\nleading the charge.\r\n\r\nResults for companies including JPMorgan Chase (JPM) and Bank of\r\nAmerica (BAC) are critical because banks effectively act as the\r\nplumbing for the U.S. economy. Investors will be laser-focused on\r\nloan demand, credit card spending, and, perhaps most importantly,\r\ndelinquency rates. Are consumers keeping up with their bills? Are\r\nbusinesses still borrowing to grow? Against forecasts for\r\neconomic reacceleration in 2026, this information will help us\r\ngauge reality.\r\n\r\nWe\xe2\x80\x99re also scheduled to receive the latest Consumer Price Index\r\n(CPI) and Producer Price Index (PPI) data, which will go a long\r\nway in refereeing the debate about whether inflation is truly\r\nvanquished.\r\n\r\nA soft inflation reading would support the camp of Federal\r\nReserve officials who want to keep cutting interest rates this\r\nyear, while a hot number could hand the hawks the evidence they\r\nneed to push for a pause.Read&nbsp;more (\r\nhttps://ablink.newsletter.sofi.com/ls/click?upn=u001.k-2FImUMyJvquSjfrastfytIPj6h1-2BVfWjnUDDp-2FeYQFLpjhu-2F6ot-2F5dL1x7ZEqHyiUNGtzFkjjgTRgCH88hkgWT22B5jOlxdZRMhlZE7Og1Lt2UyFWoW2xKeiOaRCJS5xOWmnby9rmGoslAiDrUU9HMrSPVapevjsxlq5JOOGUrhpEqF2f0-2BgBtBuKusgNd5SIC4sJgsts-2FRnfek-2BzdW-2Br4A70O-2FO-2FfgWgR1YJUP-2BdiSye5kjNi3W9N8J358I-2FreFEsbsug3eHmLY3mnWlR1Z4g-3D-3DWLZn_Hvirw9jLOYnqUg7ucIAtvDa4JCWhfjePQT8cfdKWfeKaKK6WyzUu0MT5hcL83ezarLAJeFzEeRP-2BR0XWfLa6c8fW4zxj7ovS1-2F9EYoTLeh3rS6R6GB21HU-2F-2BKGf2kGg3huojcJjHjIWym8yXEFQzyjdVNvPVLWptUsutYOyDtPyLKqFOZHB3DzkA72fs1ui-2BxW6IL7IFAtP-2B6M8n-2FZdFpSok7h648zdKIRXJ0jr-2FIj4Dqe5s7FPePjFi5guyqA78kERb8VyHNK1mKk6-2Fkk1c2oifBDjvkWpwE-2Fy1GIiVnCZXla4dDzCnU4NzP6gfwFVr1Dfd1ODHGHdrZ0m1lGN9dJjt7DwVysHhP8ktMd4b5R-2BLXw68cVB8J7FEDNxZns3nJave4XLg6lvpR2zyF-2BNPaZU2hCTj7SbQQtjFPO82jyNB0woDEslpCDaHTQueKHgJZfHesYFQcF-2BOrAgnS4OWH7fGQzQi-2BxFtSjNGggZYoAOSWNsOsGimzITOPlGhrs2VjWVHynC2dptICsXoe-2Ba20A4ghNYUoqfKtm9PUuoy898coqQgfeaIaNeBUUthVOxKIvp4kvzAudxZ-2FLJ6vbAUNENYqxuZT7EmruaRbuIRR6if6k4hBVCiZJ9m5Ppm7LlhKfAvX3c5ITFFtkGqde-2BPqVXdAoLmGQfYl4B6JjNGO9GljLCz3PVNylq8rtjCd7XzC4ruYxpZmDfuV0IonpgLKip0A4T5AxMTIY43B4zXB-2FXKU1K-2BeGuRNkZcMgeSh26-2B )PICTURE&nbsp;THIS\r\nA New Baseline for Mortgage\r\nRates?Those COVID-era bargains may be\r\nlong gone, but mortgage rates have stayed well below their\r\npost-pandemic peak for a while now. At 6.16% as of last week, the\r\naverage 30-year fixed rate on a new mortgage is down over 1.5\r\npercentage points since its October 2023 high and could stay in\r\nthat ballpark for years \r\n( https://ablink.newsletter.sofi.com/ls/click?upn=u001.k-2FImUMyJvquSjfrastfytIPj6h1-2BVf