[2026-04-27 10:34:46] Raw text/plain payload: b'2025 in review, searching for axions, spintronics, and more  \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nView as a webpage / Share [ https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/USDOEOS/bulletins/4048d52 ]\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n2025 Office of Science/DOE logo \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nRNU No Line \r\n\r\nJanuary 15, 2026\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nThe bank of cabinets of the Aurora supercomputer, decorated with its name and abstract shapes [ https://www.energy.gov/science/articles/2025-office-science-year-review-advances-discovery-science?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n2025 Office of Science Year in Review: Advances in Discovery Science\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nFor the Department of Energy\xe2\x80\x99s (DOE) Office of Science, 2025 was a year of unveiling scientific instruments and developing plans for future discoveries. In a variety of scientific fields, researchers supported by the DOE Office of Science made major advances.\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nLearn more about the Office of Science\xe2\x80\x99s 2025 accomplishments [ https://www.energy.gov/science/articles/2025-office-science-year-review-advances-discovery-science?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] in artificial intelligence, supercomputing, Nobel awards, quantum science, astrophysics, and fusion.\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nNEWS CENTER\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*Axions:* Physicists proposed the idea of an axion as a fundamental particle in the 1970s. Axions\' existence could lead to major insights into problems such as dark matter. However, they have never been detected. A team including researchers from DOE\xe2\x80\x99s Argonne National Laboratory have made significant steps towards developing a new method for hunting for axions [ https://www.anl.gov/article/hunting-a-basic-building-block-of-the-universe?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]. The researchers found a way to harness quasiparticles (particles that act as a single unit) to search for dark matter and develop new quantum technologies.\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*Frustrated magnets and AI:* Frustrated magnets are advanced materials where the electron spins do not settle on an orientation. These materials have unique properties that could be useful for energy and information technology applications. A researcher at DOE\xe2\x80\x99s Brookhaven National Laboratory who participated in the DOE\xe2\x80\x99s AI Jam Session in 2025 recently published a paper on work he started at that event. With the help of artificial intelligence, he solved a specific frustrated magnet model [ https://www.bnl.gov/newsroom/news.php?a=122742&utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] that had previously been unsolved.\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*Measuring conductivity: *Warm dense matter forms at extreme temperatures and pressures, such as at the center of stars. It\xe2\x80\x99s also relevant to the process of nuclear fusion. Understanding warm dense matter\xe2\x80\x99s ability to conduct electricity would be very helpful for understanding its physics. However, traditional methods of measuring conductivity require the measurement tool touch the material. Researchers at DOE\xe2\x80\x99s SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory developed a new way to measure electrical conductivity in this type of matter [ https://www6.slac.stanford.edu/news/2025-12-18-slac-researchers-measure-how-materials-hotter-suns-surface-conduct-electricity?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] without contact. The team used the Linac Coherent Light Source, a DOE Office of Science User Facility, to develop the new method. *\xc2\xa0*\xc2\xa0*\xc2\xa0*\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*Spintronic materials:* Spintronic materials could potentially use the spin of electrons to store and transmit data. They could be very helpful in advanced computing and memory devices. A team that includes scientists at DOE\xe2\x80\x99s Ames National Laboratory found a feature in an alloy that could lead to developing a new class of spintronic materials [ https://www.ameslab.gov/news/recent-research-reveals-pathways-for-designing-advanced-spintronic-materials?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]. This feature allows scientists to read and control spin-based signals electrically. Previously, scientists had mainly observed this feature in much simpler samples. In the potential new class of materials, it would take much less energy to manipulate the electrons\xe2\x80\x99 spins. \xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*Wildfires:* Researchers at the University of California, Irvine have identified the chief driver of the most destructive wildfires [ https://news.uci.edu/2026/01/02/uc-irvine-scientists-discover-what-drives-californias-worst-fire-years/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]. They found that fire complexes \xe2\x80\x93 clusters of fires ignited by lightning \xe2\x80\x93 cause the most destruction. When fires in a complex merge, they can produce atmospheric conditions that lead to extreme fire behavior. This behavior can then trigger even more fires and make it harder to suppress the current ones. This information could help agencies better manage such fires and prioritize limited resources. \xc2\xa0*\xc2\xa0*\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*Plant genes*: Even though scientists can edit genes in a plant with relative ease now, it is still hard to know how to revise a specific gene to lead to a specific trait. Researchers at the Joint BioEnergy Institute at DOE\xe2\x80\x99s Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory have developed a new technology that should make that process easier [ https://newscenter.lbl.gov/2025/12/18/scientists-build-speed-scanner-to-test-thousands-of-plant-gene-switches-at-once/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]. ENTRAP-seq can scan thousands of the proteins that affect how a gene is expressed in a trait. It greatly speeds up the process of investigating and controlling gene expression in plants.\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nSCIENCE HIGHLIGHTS\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*Unlocking antimony\xe2\x80\x99s potential:* Antimony-119 is a radioactive isotope that is promising for use in targeted cancer therapy. However, there is a lack of molecules that could tightly hold antimony in the body. These binding molecules are necessary for the isotope to be able to target and destroy cancer cells. Researchers at DOE\xe2\x80\x99s Oak Ridge National Laboratory and University of Wisconsin Madison have identified a binding molecule [ https://www.energy.gov/science/ip/articles/progress-towards-unlocking-antimonys-cancer-treatment-potential?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] that can serve this purpose. It forms a highly stable, protective molecular cage around the antimony when tested in mice and human blood. It\xe2\x80\x99s an important step towards using antimony in medical applications.\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\nTwo images showing mice injected with radioactive antimony. The one on the right has it much more spread out than the one on the left. [ https://www.energy.gov/science/ip/articles/progress-towards-unlocking-antimonys-cancer-treatment-potential?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nIN THE NEWS\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*"WBUR: "**Vera C. Rubin Observatory set to usher in a new era of cosmic discovery* [ https://www.wbur.org/hereandnow/2025/12/23/vera-c-rubin-observatory?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n\xc5\xbdeljko Ivezic, director of construction for the NSF-DOE Vera C. Rubin Observatory, describes what insights into astrophysics the observatory will provide.\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*"Scientific American:" **Scientists measure the temperature of the universe just after the Big Bang* [ https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/scientists-create-3-3-trillion-degree-particle-soup-to-mimic-the-universe/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\nThe Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider, a DOE Office of Science User Facility at Brookhaven National Laboratory, has taken its final measurements of the quark-gluon plasma. \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*"Gizmodo: "**Clouds are weirder at the top than we thought* [ https://gizmodo.com/clouds-are-weirder-at-the-top-than-we-thought-2000702935?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\nResearchers from DOE\xe2\x80\x99s Brookhaven National Laboratory developed a new type of remote sensing device that better captures the fine details of cloud structures. \r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nBASIC TO BREAKTHROUGH\r\n\r\n*Using AI to Usher in a New Era of Nuclear Power*\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nPhoto of the Diablo Canyon nuclear power plant [ https://www.ornl.gov/news/frontier-supercomputer-ushers-new-era-nuclear-ai?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nWith demands for electricity higher than ever, nuclear power is on the rise. Building new reactors and renewing licenses can involve huge amounts of documentation. Tech startup company Atomic Canyon and Diablo Canyon (California\xe2\x80\x99s only currently operational nuclear power plant) used the Frontier supercomputer at DOE\xe2\x80\x99s Oak Ridge Leadership Computing Facility to develop new artificial intelligence models to help them sort through records [ https://www.ornl.gov/news/frontier-supercomputer-ushers-new-era-nuclear-ai?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]. (OLCF is a DOE Office of Science User Facility.) These models will reduce the time, labor, and resources to sort through billions of pages of complex documents. They are also available to anyone in the nuclear industry to use. As the models can search the entire history of the U.S. nuclear industry, they required Frontier\xe2\x80\x99s vast computing power to develop. \xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nEND NOTES\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nDOE *National Laboratories\xe2\x80\x99 Top Research Stories of 2025 * \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nImage of an observatory on a cliff with a very clear night sky with the Milky Way in the background [ https://newscenter.lbl.gov/2025/12/17/berkeley-labs-big-science-stories-of-2025/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nThe DOE Office of Science\xe2\x80\x99s National Laboratories had quite a year of discoveries, ranging from new insights into dark energy to further expanding quantum technology. Here\xe2\x80\x99s just a selection of the accomplishments:\r\n\r\n\r\n  * Oak Ridge National Laboratory [ https://www.ornl.gov/news/2025-review-ornls-top-science-news-stories?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]: Researchers took steps forward on protecting quantum networks, announced new supercomputers, and found new applications for artificial intelligence. \r\n  * Fermilab National Accelerator Laboratory [ https://news.fnal.gov/2025/12/fermilabs-accomplishments-highlight-discovery-and-innovation-in-2025/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]: Scientists made progress on the largest neutrino experiment in the world, revealed results from the Muon g-2 experiment, and demonstrated superconducting systems that could lead to better quantum computers. \r\n  * Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory [ https://newscenter.lbl.gov/2025/12/17/berkeley-labs-big-science-stories-of-2025/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]: The laboratory led the announcement of new results from the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument that suggest that dark energy may evolve over time, launched a new open-access dataset of 100 million molecular snapshots, and developed a new method for producing the heaviest elements on the Periodic Table. \r\n  * Argonne National Laboratory [ https://www.anl.gov/article/what-were-argonnes-top-science-research-breakthroughs-in-2025?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]: The laboratory introduced the new Aurora exascale computer, set a world record with its upgraded Advanced Photon Source, and enabled the discovery of a promising HIV vaccine candidate.\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nResearch News Update\xc2\xa0provides a review of recent Office of Science Communications and Public Affairs stories and features. Please see the archive [ https://www.energy.gov/science/listings/research-news-update-archive?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] on Energy.gov for past issues.\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nNo. 148: 15 January 2026\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n  \r\n \r\n\r\nStay Connected with US Department of Energy Office of Science: Office of Science Website [ https://www.energy.gov/science/office-science?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0Twitter [ https://twitter.com/doescience?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0LinkedIn Icon [ https://www.linkedin.com/showcase/office-of-science?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0Youtube [ https://www.youtube.com/user/doescience?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]  SUBSCRIBER SERVICES:\r\nManage Subscriptions [ https://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/USDOEOS/subscriber/edit?preferences=true#tab1 ] \xc2\xa0|\xc2\xa0 Unsubscribe All [ https://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/USDOEOS/subscriber/one_click_unsubscribe?verification=5.f79aa68a3024cd2fce56df538b9b073f&destination=jasonnorman66994%40gmail.com ] \xc2\xa0|\xc2\xa0 Help [ https://subscriberhelp.govdelivery.com/ ]  \r\nbody .abe-section-table {max-width:601px !important;} body .abe-column-block {min-height: 5px;} \r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n This email was sent to jasonnorman66994@gmail.com using GovDelivery Communications Cloud on behalf of: US Department of Energy Office of Science \xc2\xb7 1000 Independence Ave., SW\xc2\xa0\xc2\xb7\xc2\xa0Washington, DC\xc2\xa0\xc2\xb7 20585\xc2\xa0\xc2\xb7\xc2\xa0(202) 586-5430  GovDelivery logo [ https://subscriberhelp.granicus.com/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n \r\nbody .abe-column-block { min-height: 5px; } table.gd_combo_table img {margin-left:10px; margin-right:10px;} table.gd_combo_table div.govd_image_display img, table.gd_combo_table td.gd_combo_image_cell img {margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px;}\r\n'
