calibri, candara, segoe, optima, arial, sans-serif; } div.rss_item div.rss_description p {color: #003d66; font-size:14px; line-height:1.3; margin:0 0 15px 0;} div.rss_item div.rss_description h1, div.rss_item div.rss_description h2, div.rss_item div.rss_description h3 { line-height:1.1 !important; } @media only screen and (max-device-width: 480px) { table#yahoo, table#yahoo table { position: relative; } table#yahoo .govd_header { max-width: 480px !important; } table#yahoo .govd_hr img, table#yahoo .govd_hr_inner img { display:none !important; } table#yahoo img { max-width: 400px !important; width: 100% !important; } table#yahoo .govd_template_image { height: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important; width: auto !important; margin:0 !important; } table#yahoo table.gd_combo_table .govd_template_image { margin:0 auto 10px auto !important; display:block !important; float:none !important; text-align:center; } table#yahoo table.gd_combo_table td.gd_combo_image_cell .govd_template_image { margin:0 !important; text-align:left; } table#yahoo #main-header .govd_template_image { max-width: 480px !important; width: 100% !important; } table#yahoo .govd_content_container .govd_image_display { margin-left:0 !important; } table#yahoo .container-fill, table.gd_tbl_wrap, table.gd_tbl_wrap table { max-width: 480px !important; } .main-table, .mobile-footer, .mobile-tagline, table.gd_tbl_wrap, table.gd_tbl_wrap table { width:100% !important; min-width:100% !important; max-width:480px !important; } table#yahoo .tablecell, .tablecell { width:100% !important; display: block !important; max-width:100% !important; min-width: 100% !important; } table#yahoo p, table#yahoo p span, table#yahoo .gd_p, table#yahoo li { font-size:13pt !important; } table#yahoo #view-in-browser p, table#yahoo #view-in-browser p span, table#yahoo #view-in-browser .gd_p, table#yahoo #view-in-browser li { font-size:8pt !important; } table#yahoo h1 { font-size:18pt !important; } table#yahoo h2 { font-size:16pt !important; } table#yahoo h3 { font-size:14pt !important; } table#yahoo #main-body .govd_hr {background:transparent none !important;} .mobile-hide {display:none !important;} .mobile-padding {padding:0 !important;} .mobile-columns {padding:10px 20px !important;} } --> \r\n\r\nEmail not displaying correctly? View it in your browser. [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/stronger-fundamentals-new-challenges-greeces-sovereign-debt ]\r\n\r\n European Financial Stability Facility Stronger fundamentals, new challenges: Greece's sovereign debt [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/stronger-fundamentals-new-challenges-greeces-sovereign-debt ] 27 January 2026 ________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nNew ESM blog post \r\n\r\nBy Matthias Gnewuch [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/profile/matthias-gnewuch ].\r\n\r\nFollowing years of fragmentation after the euro sovereign debt crisis, European bond markets have reintegrated since 2019. For countries like Greece, integration is a major success, bringing favourable financing conditions, but also new risks.\r\n\r\nDuring the crisis, Greek sovereign yields soared above those of European peers as government bond markets fragmented. In recent years, Greece\xe2\x80\x99s sovereign yields have converged with many of its peers, reflecting the strong recovery of the Greek economy and fiscal surpluses. This suggests that Greek and highly-rated European sovereign bonds are once again perceived as substitutes, signalling a renewed integration of bond markets. This renewed integration exposes Greece to different spillovers from abroad. For instance, the German fiscal expansion announced in March 2025 raised yields not only in Germany but also by a similar magnitude in Greece, highlighting the need to continuously monitor evolving risks to sovereign financing conditions.\r\n\r\n*Read the blog post\xc2\xa0**here* [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/resurgence-greek-sovereign-debt ]\r\n\r\n [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/resurgence-greek-sovereign-debt ]\r\n\r\nCheck out our other blog posts [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/ ] and columns [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/Column ].\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nDear subscribers\r\n\r\nWe\xe2\x80\x99re updating how you receive ESM blog posts. To continue getting insights on Europe\xe2\x80\x99s economy and financial stability, please confirm your subscription today.\r\n\r\n\xe2\x9c\x85 Quick and easy \xe2\x80\x93 just add your email address.\r\n\r\n\xe2\x9c\x85 Stay informed \xe2\x80\x93 receive 1\xe2\x80\x932 emails per month with expert analysis capital markets, sovereign debt, and global economic trends.\r\n\r\n?? Click here to subscribe to future ESM blog posts in 2026 onwards. [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/newsletter-blog ]\r\n\r\nHere are some of our most-read posts last year:\r\n\r\n\r\n  * When every word matters: the importance of crisis communication [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/when-every-word-matters-importance-crisis-communication ] \r\n  * Navigating quantitative tightening: funding Europe\xe2\x80\x99s future without rekindling the sovereign-bank nexus [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/navigating-quantitative-tightening-funding-europes-future-without-rekindling-sovereign-bank ] \r\n  * The butterfly effect of geopolitical conflicts [ https://www.esm.europa.eu/blog/butterfly-effect-of-geopolitical-conflicts ] \r\n\r\nAct now \xe2\x80\x93 changes take effect this month. Thank you for staying informed with ESM.\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n*Contact the **ESM Blog* <Blog@esm.europa.eu>\r\n\r\n*About ESM*\r\n\r\nThe European Stability Mechanism (ESM) has the mandate to preserve financial stability in the euro area by providing financial assistance to member states with severe financing problems. It is a permanent inter-governmental institution, operating since October 2012. The shareholders of the ESM are the 20 euro area member states. The ESM finances its assistance by issuing bonds and other debt instruments. It has a total subscribed capital of approximately \xe2\x82\xac708 billion, which comprises \xe2\x82\xac81 billion in paid-in capital and \xe2\x82\xac627 billion in committed callable capital. The ESM\xe2\x80\x99s maximum lending capacity is \xe2\x82\xac500 billion.\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nYou can unsubscribe from this newsletter anytime [ https://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/EUESM/subscriber/unsubscribe_from_topic?verification=5.9186436a11da1df6ccf021f0946092b8&destination=jasonnorman66994%40gmail.com&topic_id=EUESM_4 ]\r\n\r\n \r\nyoutube [ https://www.youtube.com/user/esmvideochannel ] twitter [ https://twitter.com/ESM_Press ] linkedin [ https://www.linkedin.com/company/european-stability-mechanism ] linkedin \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThis email was sent to jasonnorman66994@gmail.com using GovDelivery Communications Cloud, on behalf of: European Stability Mechanism \xc2\xb7 6a Circuit de la Foire, Internationale,\xc2\xa0L-1347,\xc2\xa0Luxembourg  GovDelivery logo [ https://subscriberhelp.granicus.com/ ] \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n"
