[2026-04-27 10:58:17] Raw text/plain payload: b'February Happenings at Belleplain State Forest  \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nNJStateParklogo  \r\n\xc2\xa0 February Happenings at\r\n\xc2\xa0 Belleplain State Forest\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*"News and Events from Belleplain State Forest"*\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nFeb. 1: "Full Moon Hike" \r\n\r\nFull Moon Hike at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nFull Moon Hike at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nJoin us Sunday, February 1 at 7 p.m. for Belleplain State Forest\xe2\x80\x99s most popular event \xe2\x80\x93 The Monthly Full Moon Hike!\r\n\r\nWear appropriate clothes and shoes. Bring plenty of water, snacks and bug spray! The hikes are not ADA accessible. Restrooms are available at the park office. The hike is free and open to the public. All ages are welcome. Dogs on leash welcome.\r\n\r\nIf you are planning on bringing a flashlight, please make certain that it emits red light only. White light flashlights will not be permitted as it gives us night blindness making it difficult to see the wonders of the forest in moonlight. Red glow sticks will be for sale in the park office prior to the hike.\r\n\r\nPlease also note this hike is our most well attended, we try to start as close to the start time as possible. To ensure we stay on schedule, please arrive at least 15 minutes early to use restrooms, buy glow sticks and/or exclusive full moon hike stickers.\r\n\r\nRegistration is not required.\r\n\r\nThe hike will be cancelled for clouds or extremely bad weather. Watch for updates on our Facebook [ https://www.facebook.com/belleplainstateforest/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] page.\r\n\r\n"We will meet at the Belleplain State Forest Office at 1 Henkinsifkin Road, Woodbine, NJ 08270."\r\n\r\n"For more information, call the park office at 609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nFeb. 12: "Thursday Trails" \r\n\r\nA Thursday in the forest at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nA Thursday along the shores of Lake Nummy at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nJoin us on Thursday, February 12 at 10 a.m. for one of our every other Thursday hikes where we will take you on various length trails around Belleplain to get you out and about in our beautiful forest! Hikes are typically 2.5-3.5 miles.\r\n\r\nWear appropriate clothes and shoes. Bring plenty of water, snacks and bug spray! The hikes are not ADA accessible. Restrooms are available at the park office. The hike is free and open to the public. All ages are welcome. Dogs on leash welcome.\r\n\r\nRegistration is not required.\r\n\r\nThe hike will be cancelled for extremely bad weather. Watch for updates on our Facebook page\r\n\r\n"We will meet at the Belleplain State Forest Office at 1 Henkinsifkin Road, Woodbine, NJ 08270."\r\n\r\n"For more information, email ""Gina.Morgan@dep.nj.gov" <Gina.Morgan@dep.nj.gov>" or call the park office at 609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nFeb. 13: "Galentine\'s Day Hike and Social" \r\n\r\nGalentine\'s treats at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nGalentine\'s treats at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nGrab your gal pals and hiking boots to kick off your Valentines Day weekend celebrations here at Belleplain! No gal pal? No problem! Make new ones here at Belleplain State Forest!\r\n\r\nJoin us on Friday, February 13 at 10 a.m. for a short hike (2-2.5 miles) followed by Valentine\xe2\x80\x99s Day themed treats and mocktails in the conference room!\r\n\r\nWear appropriate clothes and shoes for the hike. Consider bringing bug spray if the temperature is above freezing. The hike is not ADA accessible. Restrooms are available at the park office. This event is free and open to the public. All ages are welcome.\r\n\r\n"We will meet at the Belleplain State Forest Office at 1 Henkinsifkin Road, Woodbine, NJ 08270."\r\n\r\n"Registration is required and can be made by calling the Belleplain State Forest office at \xc2\xa0609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nFeb. 14: "Make A Heart Shaped Bird Feeder" \r\n\r\nHeart-Shaped Bird Feeder \r\n\r\nHeart-Shaped Bird Feeder\r\n\r\nStop by the Belleplain State Forest office any time between 12 p.m. and 2 p.m. on Saturday, February 14 to make a heart shaped bird feeder!\r\n\r\nAll ages can make a bird feeder (kids must be accompanied by an adult).\r\n\r\nBird seed mixture will be premade and provided. "Please note, the bird feeders have to be frozen in order for the shape to set. You can leave your bird feeder at the office and come back to get it."\r\n\r\nSupplies will be LIMITED!\r\n\r\n$5 donation per feeder recommended\r\n\r\nEmail Gina.Morgan@dep.nj.gov or call the Belleplain State Forest office at 609-861-2404 with any questions.\r\n\r\n"We will meet at the Belleplain State Forest Office at 1 Henkinsifkin Road, Woodbine, NJ 08270."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nFeb. 19: "Thursday Trails" \r\n\r\nHiking in Belleplain State Forest on a Winter Thursday \r\n\r\nHiking in Belleplain State Forest on a Winter Thursday\r\n\r\nJoin us on Thursday, February 19 at 10 a.m. at the Nature Center for one of our every other Thursday hikes where we will take you on various length trails around Belleplain to get you out and about in our beautiful forest! Hikes are typically 2.5-3.5 miles.\r\n\r\nWe will meet at the Bellplain State Forest Nature Center.\r\n\r\nWear appropriate clothes and shoes. Bring plenty of water, snacks and bug spray! The hikes are not ADA accessible. Restrooms are available at the park office. The hike is free and open to the public. All ages are welcome. Dogs on leash welcome.\r\n\r\nRegistration is not required.\r\n\r\nThe hike will be cancelled for extremely bad weather. Watch for updates on our Facebook [ https://www.facebook.com/belleplainstateforest/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] page.\r\n\r\n"We will meet at the Bellplain State Forest Nature Center."\r\n\r\n"For more information, email ""Gina.Morgan@dep.nj.gov" <Gina.Morgan@dep.nj.gov>" or call the park office at 609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nFeb. 20: "SJAC Public Skywatch" \r\n\r\nPublic Skywatch at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nThe moon above Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nDid you know that Belleplain State Forest is one of the darkest spots in Cape May County and is recognized as a Bortle Class 5 dark sky location?\r\n\r\nJoin the South Jersey Astronomy Club [ https://sjac.us/events-activities/calendar-of-events-2026/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] (SJAC) on Friday, February 20 at 7:30 p.m. for a "FREE" public SkyWatch in Belleplain State Forest\'s athletic field. The first one of 2026!\r\n\r\nTelescopes will be set up to give beautiful views of objects in the night sky! Just walk up to any telescope and ask a SJAC member what they are viewing and if you can take a look.\r\n\r\nThe SkyWatch is weather dependent. If the sky is cloudy, the event will either be moved to the next day (Saturday, February 21st) or cancelled. Watch for updates on our Facebook [ https://www.facebook.com/belleplainstateforest/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] page.\r\n\r\n"For more information, call the park office at 609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nFeb. 21: "Dog Hike" \r\n\r\nOut for a hike with our favorite friends at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nOut for a hike with our favorite friends at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nJoin Megan and her furry companions on Saturday, February 21 at 10 a.m. at the park office for a moderately paced dog hike!\r\n\r\nThe hike will be anywhere from 2.5 - 4 miles.\r\n\r\nAll dogs must be leashed (6 feet or less in length). Remember to pick up after your pet!\r\n\r\nFor more information, call the park office at 609-861-2404.\r\n\r\nThe hike will be cancelled for clouds or extremely bad weather. Watch for updates on our Facebook [ https://www.facebook.com/belleplainstateforest/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] page.\r\n\r\n"We will meet at the Belleplain State Forest Office at 1 Henkinsifkin Road, Woodbine, NJ\xc2\xa0 08270."\r\n\r\n"For more information, call the park office at 609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nNaturalist\'s Corner: "Messages in the Snow" \r\n\r\nTracks in the snow of the grey squirrel and the cottontail rabbit \r\n\r\nTracks in the snow of the grey squirrel and the cottontail rabbit\r\n\r\nGaze upon freshly fallen snow and the winter landscape appears quiet and peaceful. Nature seems to be on hold with the beloved animal kingdom fast asleep.\r\n\r\nBut in taking a closer look, the blanket of snow tells a different story. For animals that neither migrate or hibernate, winter is a time to hustle for food and shelter. It is no time to stand still. A covering of snow becomes a book filled with anecdotes, messages and clues with many, not all, written clearly in the language of animal tracks.\r\n\r\nOnly a little sleuthing is needed to reconstruct events that occurred earlier in the snow. But to read the story clearly, it is best that snow be wet and no more than three inches deep. It is also helpful to know that there are three basic clues to look for when identifying tracks: the habitat where the track was found, the actual track pattern, and the presence of any auxiliary signs.\r\n\r\nThe gray squirrel lives in deciduous trees, particularly those that bear nuts. If you see small, \xe2\x80\x9cfour-print\xe2\x80\x9d tracks (a group of four footprints followed by another group of four) leading to or from such a tree, then the habitat clue suggests squirrel prints. Cracked nut shells or eaten pinecones are good auxiliary evidence, as is a leaf nest within the tree.\r\n\r\nThe four-print track of a hopping cottontail rabbit looks as if it were put together backwards. The two-side-by-side marks from the rabbit\xe2\x80\x99s large hind feet come first, showing where the animal landed. Just behind come two smaller front foot marks, indicating where the rabbit pushed off. Gnaw marks on the trunks of shrubs or on trees in brushy fields provide another clue.\r\n\r\nRed fox tracks in the snow \r\n\r\nRed fox tracks in the snow\r\n\r\nRed foxes leave a doglike paw print in a straight line, usually across open fields where the animals prefer to hunt. Each print is rounded and may have impressions from foot pads and toenails. Auxiliary signs left by red foxes include urine marks and doglike scat with a twist at the end.\r\n\r\nWhite-tailed deer track in the snow \r\n\r\nWhite-tailed deer tracks in the snow\r\n\r\nThe white-tailed deer leaves a sharp-pointed, heart-shaped track. The two large front pads of the hoof may be followed by two dewclaw marks in the rear of each footprint. Most deer drag their feet, particularly in the deep snow.\r\n\r\nTherefore, when you are taking that glorious walk after a fresh snowfall this month, enjoy the clues and signs of our brother and sister animals that are out exploring and looking for a mid-winter meal. Winter makes an excellent season for reading the daily wildlife news written in tracks. Enjoy the adventure!\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nWe\'re Hiring for Summer 2026\r\n\r\nA seasonal employee at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nA seasonal employee at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nLove the outdoors? Want a job with a view of the lake? We\xe2\x80\x99ll soon be hiring for seasonal park positions for the upcoming 2026 season! It\xe2\x80\x99s the perfect opportunity to spend summer outside and make an impact in the park you love.\r\n\r\nCheck out what is available now and start planning for summer 2026 [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/jobs/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]!\r\n\r\n"For more information, please contact the Belleplain State Forest office at 609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nCelebrate 250 Challenge\r\n\r\nCelebrate 250 Challenge \r\n\r\nJoin Belleplain State Forest in the 250 Challenge\r\n\r\nGet outdoors, get moving, and celebrate the United States\xe2\x80\x99 250th anniversary in 2026 with the New Jersey State Parks Celebrate 250 Challenge. This yearlong challenge encourages visitors of all ages and abilities to explore New Jersey\xe2\x80\x99s 41 state parks, 11 state forests, five recreation areas, and 50+ historic sites and districts while building healthy habits and honoring this milestone year.\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nAll participants who successfully complete\xc2\xa0one of the five challenge options will earn\xc2\xa0a\xc2\xa0Certificate of\xc2\xa0Achievement\xc2\xa0and the opportunity to be\xc2\xa0recognized\xc2\xa0on New Jersey State Parks\xe2\x80\x99 social media.\r\n\r\n\r\n  * *250 Miles in a Year*: For the avid hiker, biker, or walker, average just under five miles per week at any New Jersey state park, forest\xc2\xa0or historic site. \r\n  * *25.0 Miles in a Year*: Perfect for casual visitors, average two miles per month hiking or walking at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. \r\n  * *25.0 Different Trails in a Year*: Explore our trails at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. \r\n  * *2.50 Miles per Week*: Can be achieved at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. A steady, approachable pace totaling about 130 miles in a year. \r\n  * *25.0 Minutes per Week Outdoors*: For all ages and abilities, simply celebrate by spending time outside at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0Are you ready to take the challenge? [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/get-outside/celebrate-250-challenge/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n*About the United States\xe2\x80\x99 250th Anniversary*\r\n\r\nThroughout 2026, New Jersey\xe2\x80\x99s state parks, forests, and historic sites will celebrate the nation\xe2\x80\x99s 250th anniversary with a variety of public programs and events. Join in on the fun as we explore New Jersey\xe2\x80\x99s history and learn how the events, challenges and ideas of the past continue to shape our nation today. From guided First Day Hikes to Revolutionary War reenactments and themed programs, there is truly something for everyone.\xc2\xa0Learn more\xc2\xa0 [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/discover-history/united-states-250/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]about\xc2\xa0how New Jersey State Parks is\xc2\xa0celebrating the United States\xe2\x80\x99 250th anniversary.\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nCamping at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nA lean-to at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nA lean-to at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nBelleplain State Forest offers camping all year-round! We have tent sites, electric hookup tent/trailer campsites, shelters, and lean-tos. We even have a large group cabin on our East Creek Lake. All camping information can be found and booked at New Jersey Outdoors [ https://www.njportal.com/DEP/NJOutdoors/Park/Details?locationId=4&utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ].\r\n\r\nThe water for the CCC campground has been turned "off". Water will still be available at the dump station. Make sure to fill before you arrive or fill at the dump station! Electric at the CCC campground is available all year.\r\n\r\nThe Meisle campground bathhouse is closed for renovations. There will be port-a-potties placed in the campground, but showers will only be available in the CCC campground. Campsites 231-244 are closed during the renovations.\r\n\r\n"For more information, call the park office at 609-861-2404."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nBelleplain State Forest\'s Little Free Library\r\n\r\nBelleplain State Forest\'s Free Little Library \r\n\r\nThe Free Little Library at Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nStop by the Nature Center to check out our Little Free Library!\r\n\r\nThe Little Free Library is a free book-sharing box where anyone may take a book or share a book. It functions on the honor system. You do not need to share a book in order to take one. If you take a book or two from the little library, try to bring some to share when you can.\r\n\r\nThere is always a range of children\xe2\x80\x99s books, young adult books, adult books, fiction, non-fiction, nature-based books, etc.\r\n\r\nHappy reading!\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nAbout Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nA snowy Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nA snowy Belleplain State Forest\r\n\r\nBelleplain State Forest was established in 1928 for recreation, wildlife management, timber production and water conservation. In 1933, three camps were set up by the Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC) Reforestation Relief Act, supplying labor to the forest for over an eight-year period.\r\n\r\nLocated within the Pinelands National Reserve, the more than 21,000-acre forest contains the greatest variety of habitats anywhere in New Jersey, including saltwater marsh, Atlantic white cedar swamp, mixed hardwood swamp and oak-hickory forest.\r\n\r\nThere are over forty miles of trails for hiking, biking and horseback riding. Other activities include hunting, fishing, kayaking, canoeing, paddleboarding, camping, stargazing and during the summer, swimming at Lake Nummy. Belleplain is a well-known birding spot, especially during the spring migration.\r\n\r\nVisit Belleplain State Forest\'s website [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/state-park/belleplain-state-forest/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nBelleplain State Forest Office\r\n\r\n1 Henkinsifkin Road, Woodbine, NJ 08270\r\n609-861-2404\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nThe Office is open 7 days a week 8:30 a.m. - 4 p.m.\r\n\r\n"Office hours may vary depending on staffing, events, weather, holiday closures, etc."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n  \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n*Like us on Facebook!\xc2\xa0\r\n*Belleplain State Forest | Facebook [ https://www.facebook.com/belleplainstateforest/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nNew Jersey State Parks [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nQuestions?\xc2\xa0Contact Us [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/about/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n \xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0 \r\nSign up for email updates [ https://www.facebook.com/NewJerseyStateParks?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]  [ https://twitter.com/newjerseydep?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] Follow us on Instagram [ https://www.instagram.com/newjerseystateparks/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] Sign up for email updates [ http://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/NJDEP/subscriber/new ] \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nSUBSCRIBER SERVICES:\r\n\r\nManage Preferences [ http://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/NJDEP/subscribers/new?preferences=true ] \xc2\xa0|\xc2\xa0 Unsubscribe [ https://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/NJDEP/subscribers/new?preferences=true ] \xc2\xa0|\xc2\xa0 Help [ https://subscriberhelp.govdelivery.com/ ]\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n This email was sent to jasonnorman66994@gmail.com using GovDelivery Communications Cloud on behalf of: New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection \xc2\xb7 401 E. State St. \xc2\xb7 Trenton, NJ 08625 GovDelivery logo [ https://subscriberhelp.granicus.com/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n \r\nbody .abe-column-block { min-height: 5px; } table.gd_combo_table img {margin-left:10px; margin-right:10px;} table.gd_combo_table div.govd_image_display img, table.gd_combo_table td.gd_combo_image_cell img {margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px;} table.govd_hr {min-width: 100%;}\r\n'
