[2026-04-27 10:58:19] Raw text/plain payload: b'Mosquito Bites: February 2026  \r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nNJStateParklogo  \r\n\xc2\xa0 MOSQUITO BITES:\r\n\xc2\xa0 February 2026\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n*"News and Events from Bass River State Forest"*\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nWe\'re Hiring for Summer 2026\r\n\r\nA seasonal employee at Belleplain State Forest \r\n\r\nA seasonal employee at Bass River State Forest\r\n\r\nLove the outdoors? Want a job with a view of the lake? We\xe2\x80\x99ll soon be hiring for seasonal park positions for the upcoming 2026 season! It\xe2\x80\x99s the perfect opportunity to spend summer outside and make an impact in the park you love.\r\n\r\nCheck out what is available now and start planning for summer 2026 [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/jobs/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]!\r\n\r\n"For more information, please contact the Bass River State Forest office at 609-561-0024."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nNaturalist\'s Corner: "Messages in the Snow" \r\n\r\nTracks in the snow of the grey squirrel and the cottontail rabbit \r\n\r\nA BARK Ranger at Bass River State Forest\r\n\r\nGaze upon freshly fallen snow and the winter landscape appears quiet and peaceful. Nature seems to be on hold with the beloved animal kingdom fast asleep.\r\n\r\nBut in taking a closer look, the blanket of snow tells a different story. For animals that neither migrate or hibernate, winter is a time to hustle for food and shelter. It is no time to stand still. A covering of snow becomes a book filled with anecdotes, messages and clues with many, not all, written clearly in the language of animal tracks.\r\n\r\nOnly a little sleuthing is needed to reconstruct events that occurred earlier in the snow. But to read the story clearly, it is best that snow be wet and no more than three inches deep. It is also helpful to know that there are three basic clues to look for when identifying tracks: the habitat where the track was found, the actual track pattern, and the presence of any auxiliary signs.\r\n\r\nThe gray squirrel lives in deciduous trees, particularly those that bear nuts. If you see small, \xe2\x80\x9cfour-print\xe2\x80\x9d tracks (a group of four footprints followed by another group of four) leading to or from such a tree, then the habitat clue suggests squirrel prints. Cracked nut shells or eaten pinecones are good auxiliary evidence, as is a leaf nest within the tree.\r\n\r\nThe four-print track of a hopping cottontail rabbit looks as if it were put together backwards. The two-side-by-side marks from the rabbit\xe2\x80\x99s large hind feet come first, showing where the animal landed. Just behind come two smaller front foot marks, indicating where the rabbit pushed off. Gnaw marks on the trunks of shrubs or on trees in brushy fields provide another clue.\r\n\r\nRed fox tracks in the snow \r\n\r\nRed fox tracks in the snow\r\n\r\nRed foxes leave a doglike paw print in a straight line, usually across open fields where the animals prefer to hunt. Each print is rounded and may have impressions from foot pads and toenails. Auxiliary signs left by red foxes include urine marks and doglike scat with a twist at the end.\r\n\r\nWhite-tailed deer track in the snow \r\n\r\nWhite-tailed deer tracks in the snow\r\n\r\nThe white-tailed deer leaves a sharp-pointed, heart-shaped track. The two large front pads of the hoof may be followed by two dewclaw marks in the rear of each footprint. Most deer drag their feet, particularly in the deep snow.\r\n\r\nTherefore, when you are taking that glorious walk after a fresh snowfall this month, enjoy the clues and signs of our brother and sister animals that are out exploring and looking for a mid-winter meal. Winter makes an excellent season for reading the daily wildlife news written in tracks. Enjoy the adventure!\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nCamping at Bass River State Forest\r\n\r\nBass River State Forest\'s Lean-to 8 \r\n\r\nLean-to 8 on a snowy day at Bass River State Forest\r\n\r\nCamping is available at Bass River State Forest year-round, no matter the season. As winter approaches, it\xe2\x80\x99s time to stay cozy by the fire! Come make s\xe2\x80\x99mores, drink hot chocolate, and enjoy the crisp winter air at one of our campsites or lean-tos.\r\n\r\nCampsites and lean-tos on our South Shore are open year-round while campsites and cabins on the North Shore close for the season\xc2\xa0after October 31 and will reopen Memorial Day weekend.\r\n\r\nWe offer pet friendly camping, bathroom amenities, picnic pavilions, fishing, wildlife views, and swimming. Swimming is only permitted, however, during the summer season and when lifeguards are on duty.\r\n\r\nOnline reservations and additional information can be found at https://camping.nj.gov [ https://www.njportal.com/DEP/NJOutdoors/Park/Details?locationId=3&utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]. Make your winter camping reservations today!\r\n\r\n"Bass River State Forest is located at 762 Stage Road, Tuckerton, NJ 08087."\r\n\r\n" For more information, please call the office at 609-296-1114."\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nCelebrate 250 Challenge\r\n\r\nCelebrate 250 Challenge \r\n\r\nJoin Bass River State Forest in the 250 Challenge\r\n\r\nGet outdoors, get moving, and celebrate the United States\xe2\x80\x99 250th anniversary in 2026 with the New Jersey State Parks Celebrate 250 Challenge. This yearlong challenge encourages visitors of all ages and abilities to explore New Jersey\xe2\x80\x99s 41 state parks, 11 state forests, five recreation areas, and 50+ historic sites and districts while building healthy habits and honoring this milestone year.\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nAll participants who successfully complete\xc2\xa0one of the five challenge options will earn\xc2\xa0a\xc2\xa0Certificate of\xc2\xa0Achievement\xc2\xa0and the opportunity to be\xc2\xa0recognized\xc2\xa0on New Jersey State Parks\xe2\x80\x99 social media.\r\n\r\n\r\n  * *250 Miles in a Year*: For the avid hiker, biker, or walker, average just under five miles per week at any New Jersey state park, forest\xc2\xa0or historic site. \r\n  * *25.0 Miles in a Year*: Perfect for casual visitors, average two miles per month hiking or walking at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. \r\n  * *25.0 Different Trails in a Year*: Explore our trails at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. \r\n  * *2.50 Miles per Week*: Can be achieved at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. A steady, approachable pace totaling about 130 miles in a year. \r\n  * *25.0 Minutes per Week Outdoors*: For all ages and abilities, simply celebrate by spending time outside at any New Jersey state park, forest or historic site. \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0Are you ready to take the challenge? [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/get-outside/celebrate-250-challenge/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n*About the United States\xe2\x80\x99 250th Anniversary*\r\n\r\nThroughout 2026, New Jersey\xe2\x80\x99s state parks, forests, and historic sites will celebrate the nation\xe2\x80\x99s 250th anniversary with a variety of public programs and events. Join in on the fun as we explore New Jersey\xe2\x80\x99s history and learn how the events, challenges and ideas of the past continue to shape our nation today. From guided First Day Hikes to Revolutionary War reenactments and themed programs, there is truly something for everyone.\xc2\xa0Learn more\xc2\xa0 [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/discover-history/united-states-250/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]about\xc2\xa0how New Jersey State Parks is\xc2\xa0celebrating the United States\xe2\x80\x99 250th anniversary.\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nAbout Bass River State Forest\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nBass River State Forest\'s Nature Center \r\n\r\nBass River State Forest\'s Nature Center on a cold and snowy day\r\n\r\nThe nearly 24,000-acre Bass River State Forest is New Jersey\xe2\x80\x99s first state forest, established in 1905 for public recreation, water conservation, wildlife, and timber management. In June 1933, Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC) Camp No. 7 was established here, and the CCC \xe2\x80\x9cboys\xe2\x80\x9d planted trees and built camping structures, picnic pavilions, and roads that are still in use today.\r\n\r\nBass River State Forest is within the Pinelands National Reserve, a one-million-acre ecologically sensitive area that is home to many rare plants and animals. This includes the rare dwarf pine plains, a globally rare plant community where, in some places, fully mature pitch pine trees reach a height of only five to six feet.\r\n\r\nBass River State Forest and its Ballanger Creek Area has more than 23 miles of trails including some multi-use trails. The southern terminus of the 53-mile-long Batona Trail is also within Bass River State Forest. From cedar swamps to pine plantations to fishing or swimming on Lake Absegami, there is plenty to see or do at Bass River State Forest whether you\xe2\x80\x99re camping or just visiting for the day.\r\n\r\nCome visit for the day or longer!\r\n\r\nVisit Bass River State Forest\'s website [ https://nj.gov/dep/parksandforests/parks/bassriverstateforest.html?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nBass River State Forest Office\r\n\r\n762 Stage Road, Tuckerton, NJ 08087\r\n609-296-1114\r\n\r\n"The Office is open 7 days a week except major holidays:"\r\n\r\n\r\n  * "Sunday - Thursday: 8:30 a.m. - 4 p.m." \r\n  * "Friday - Saturday: 8:30 a.m. - 6 p.m.\r\n" \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\n  \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n*Like us on Facebook!\xc2\xa0\r\n*Bass River State Forest | Facebook [ https://www.facebook.com/bassriverstateforest/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nNew Jersey State Parks [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\nQuestions?\xc2\xa0Contact Us [ https://dep.nj.gov/parksandforests/about/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]\r\n\r\n \xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0 \r\nSign up for email updates [ https://www.facebook.com/NewJerseyStateParks?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ]  [ https://twitter.com/newjerseydep?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] Follow us on Instagram [ https://www.instagram.com/newjerseystateparks/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] Sign up for email updates [ http://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/NJDEP/subscriber/new ] \r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nSUBSCRIBER SERVICES:\r\n\r\nManage Preferences [ http://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/NJDEP/subscribers/new?preferences=true ] \xc2\xa0|\xc2\xa0 Unsubscribe [ https://public.govdelivery.com/accounts/NJDEP/subscribers/new?preferences=true ] \xc2\xa0|\xc2\xa0 Help [ https://subscriberhelp.govdelivery.com/ ]\xc2\xa0\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n This email was sent to jasonnorman66994@gmail.com using GovDelivery Communications Cloud on behalf of: New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection \xc2\xb7 401 E. State St. \xc2\xb7 Trenton, NJ 08625 GovDelivery logo [ https://subscriberhelp.granicus.com/?utm_medium=email&utm_source=govdelivery ] \r\n \r\nbody .abe-column-block { min-height: 5px; } table.gd_combo_table img {margin-left:10px; margin-right:10px;} table.gd_combo_table div.govd_image_display img, table.gd_combo_table td.gd_combo_image_cell img {margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px;} table.govd_hr {min-width: 100%;}\r\n'
