rSQAJcWnsWssQX7S6-2FV8VQG6Y3NNTv-2FWbh9np0VrNuRDApRv-2F6rPEhDed7kiDfug2UGF-2F0954dqDmacctq6DrC8sFuKdJJh7t1xzS-2FaXdTscuyTOcC09f5CY44WJJrPPuOKahsHsVHhGDzzGc9b2dTHyj5LGy6iJ-2BikyAkFAr0TlpZMKKk4nnPk1qcMB2jFi2KZ1tJkarF7ny04MBAvLRMvrah7ZXShxWEw1iWR692AWv2ypX9A6vqYdDXNTd5JRXbKfUX\r\n)By Dawn Papandrea\r\n\r\nBuying a car should be exciting, but with new vehicles averaging\r\nover $50,000, the risks of overextending yourself have grown.\r\n\r\nThe most startling stat? The record size of car payments.\r\nFinancing a new vehicle now costs an average of $772 a month, and\r\n1 in 5 buyers is shelling out at least $1,000. Even used cars\r\noften require well over $500 a month, and to make the math work,\r\nmany buyers are stretching loans to seven years, sometimes adding\r\nthousands to their interest costs.\r\n\r\nWith auto loan delinquencies at a 15-year high, the pressure on\r\nbuyers is clear. Here are the numbers to run before you set foot\r\non the lot.Keep&nbsp;reading (\r\nhttps://ablink.newsletter.sofi.com/ls/click?upn=u001.k-2FImUMyJvquSjfrastfytIPj6h1-2BVfWjnUDDp-2FeYQFJgaQjEiLCkaMTJKxwAZUjvV3LOs9NhaZae6qJVBKBDvEaZsVthVTeez7DuuBYytP72XdvgLgIBTOQoe7QCSxkwqrMPy-2BZ4x7ZWxo8nH8iirqpJaTYHk7UnVjukYTlqtbOt3q3c2084nLX8GDubQ7cEL3krLEuLirXVB2eO7YJfl52JXqUXAS29wd6gO2kRZDyyx8VVakim0bijxYGLkaDDT9DQSDL6jBJCo2se0dUbDg-3D-3DjV7H_Hvirw9jLOYnqUg7ucIAtvDa4JCWhfjePQT8cfdKWfeKaKK6WyzUu0MT5hcL83ezarLAJeFzEeRP-2BR0XWfLa6cw3EWWwg9zywQ9XyxItpXREu8WYlcn72L837hLXTCripMk5hL-2BX20Yo1ocNGtMXmmqUBCyAztv9E0QHhuvk3rW6c-2BneJcJL-2FSERKfSMnRihCRpZDKYEXSy6S-2F0OultYOOd-2BwHqddFRpkcdnxpHZc5VI4gu0warx2QvfDsaX-2BqwUxGaUud1AbBX6sRxceJFSdi97cZqtUQEJu6Oy4OCCnLN-2Fc-2FewVhqnJOwtMEpzb-2BH0RPVZB0xCWwslmKJEms5dezE-2BJuNdwDOIEFkN0BmSuMDA2Sn3nc7cX-2BBc0LdwhnrtZquehva3DmwMt0Fit-2B-2B5mDi94qL-2BASokAt-2Bq1u7Vc6cWXdvpWkDLF638yko5PTwIt16Pp2A2vetekKFoEDaupLaM-2B2vmUzqTfjdZSY5hrXPUpfhd2yszjJ3qXFvrSQAJcWnsWssQX7S6-2FV8VQG6Y3NNTv-2FWbh9np0VrNuRDApRv-2F6rPEhDed7kiDfug2UGF-2F0954dqDmacctq6DrC8sFuKdJJh7t1xzS-2FaXdTscuyTOeQVz