[2026-04-27 11:09:53] Raw text/plain payload: b"Bookmark and Share [ https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/USIRS/bulletins/406eace?reqfrom=share ]\r\n\r\nIRS.gov Banner \r\n\r\ne-News for Tax Professionals\r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nFebruary 2, 2026\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nIssue Number: \xc2\xa02026-05\r\n\r\nInside This Issue\r\n\r\n  * \r\nIRS requests applications for 2026 ETAAC membership [ #First ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\nIRS issues FAQs about Executive Order 14247: Modernizing Payments To and From America\xe2\x80\x99s Bank Account [ #Second ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\nTreasury, IRS issue FAQs about the new deduction for qualified overtime compensation [ #Third ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\nSimple Payment Plans have expanded to businesses [ #Fourth ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\nNational Taxpayer Advocate delivers Annual Report to Congress [ #Fifth ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\n2025 Nationwide Tax Forum Online: How to help taxpayers avoid abusive tax promotions and abusive return preparers [ #Sixth ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\nReminder: VDP comment period [ #Seventh ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\nDid you know? IRS tax tips [ #Eighth ]\r\n\r\n  * \r\nTechnical guidance [ #Ninth ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*1.\xc2\xa0 IRS requests applications for 2026 ETAAC membership*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThe IRS is accepting applications [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-requests-applications-for-2026-etaac-membership ] for the Electronic Tax Administration Advisory Committee [ https://www.irs.gov/tax-professionals/electronic-tax-administration-advisory-committee-etaac ] through Feb. 28, 2026.\r\n\r\nThe ETAAC is an organized public forum for discussing issues in electronic tax administration, such as the prevention of identity theft and refund fraud. The committee supports the overriding goal that paperless filing should be the preferred and most convenient method of filing tax and information returns.\r\n\r\nETAAC members work closely with the Security Summit [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/security-summit ], a joint effort of the IRS, state tax administrators, and private-sector tax partners to fight electronic fraud and tax-related identity theft.\r\n\r\nThe IRS is seeking qualified individuals to serve three-year terms, beginning in September 2026. Tax professionals should consider applying. The IRS also strongly encourages applications from people representing the viewpoints of average taxpayers, including consumer advocates and others with an interest in tax issues.\r\n\r\nOrganizations and individuals may submit nominations of qualified individuals by letter. Applicants need to complete the ETAAC application [ https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/f13768.pdf ] and include a statement of interest and a resume. Applicants must also complete and submit a tax check waiver form and undergo an IRS practitioner background check. Applicants deemed \xe2\x80\x9cbest qualified\xe2\x80\x9d will also undergo an FBI background check. Information on the tax check waiver will be provided upon receipt of the application.\r\n\r\nETAAC is a Federal Advisory Committee established by the Internal Revenue Service Restructuring and Reform Act of 1998.\r\n\r\nQuestions about the ETAAC and the application process can be emailed to publicliaison@irs.gov.\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*2.\xc2\xa0 IRS issues FAQs about Executive Order 14247: Modernizing Payments To and From America\xe2\x80\x99s Bank Account*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThe Internal Revenue Service issued frequently asked questions [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-issues-frequently-asked-questions-about-executive-order-14247-modernizing-payments-to-and-from-americas-bank-account ] to help taxpayers, businesses, and other stakeholders understand the changes under Executive Order 14247: Modernizing Payment To and From America\xe2\x80\x99s Bank Account [ https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/03/modernizing-payments-to-and-from-americas-bank-account/ ].\r\n\r\nThese changes apply to:\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nPayments sent by the federal government, including tax refunds, benefits, grants, and vendor or contractor payments; and\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nPayments made to the federal government, including tax balances due, fees, penalties, and other payments from individuals, businesses, nonprofit organizations, and state or local partners.\r\n\r\nTax professionals should encourage their clients to:\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nUse direct deposit [ https://www.irs.gov/refunds/get-your-refund-faster-tell-irs-to-direct-deposit-your-refund-to-one-two-or-three-accounts ] for refunds by providing accurate bank or prepaid debit card information when filing.\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nChoose electronic payment options when paying taxes, such as IRS Direct Pay [ https://www.irs.gov/payments/direct-pay-with-bank-account ], Electronic Federal Tax Payment System [ https://www.irs.gov/payments/eftps-the-electronic-federal-tax-payment-system ], or other approved methods.\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nReview account information to ensure bank details are current and correct.\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nVisit IRS.gov to learn about electronic payment [ https://www.irs.gov/payments ] options and available resources for taxpayers without a bank account.\r\n\r\nFor more information about how the IRS is implementing the Executive Order, visit Modernizing Payments To and From America\xe2\x80\x99s Bank Account [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/modernizing-payments-to-and-from-americas-bank-account ] on IRS.gov.\r\n\r\nA paper check with a dinosaur claw mark through it. Text: \xe2\x80\x98Leave paper behind. IRS is phasing out paper refund checks.\xe2\x80\x99 IRS logo also displayed.\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*3.\xc2\xa0 Treasury, IRS issue FAQs about the new deduction for qualified overtime compensation*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThe Department of the Treasury and the IRS also issued frequently asked questions [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/treasury-irs-issue-faqs-to-address-the-new-deduction-for-qualified-overtime-compensation-under-the-one-big-beautiful-bill ] related to the new deduction for qualified overtime compensation under the One, Big, Beautiful Bill.\r\n\r\nFor tax years 2025 through 2028, individuals who receive qualified overtime compensation may deduct the amount that exceeds their regular rate of pay (generally, the \xe2\x80\x9chalf\xe2\x80\x9d portion of \xe2\x80\x9ctime-and-a-half\xe2\x80\x9d compensation) and is reported on a Form W-2 or Form 1099.\r\n\r\nThese FAQs contain additional information about the deduction, provide resources assist tax professionals in determining whether their employees received qualified overtime compensation under the Fair Labor Standards Act. The FAQs also contain useful information regarding the differences in reporting requirements for tax year 2025 and 2026-2028.\r\n\r\nA red block with word \xe2\x80\x9cOVERTIME\xe2\x80\x9d next to red alarm clock. Text: \xe2\x80\x98Worked overtime? You may qualify for a new tax deduction\xe2\x80\x99 IRS logo also displayed.\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*4.\xc2\xa0 Simple Payment Plans have expanded to businesses*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThe IRS recently updated qualifications for Simple Payment Plans [ https://www.irs.gov/payments/simple-payment-plans-for-individuals-and-businesses ], formerly known as \xe2\x80\x9cStreamlined Installment Agreements,\xe2\x80\x9d to include business taxpayers. Simple Payment Plans are long-term payment arrangements available for qualified taxpayers.\r\n\r\nOn Dec. 3, 2025, the IRS updated how it processes business installment agreements. Now both the In-Business Trust Fund Express Agreement and the Business Streamlined Agreement are now processed under the updated Simple Payment Plan qualifications.\r\n\r\n*General qualifications*\r\n\r\nAll applicants for a Simple Payment Plan must be current with all filing and payment requirements.\r\n\r\n*Businesses*\r\n\r\nWith trust fund taxes [ https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/trust-fund-taxes ]:\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\n$25,000 or less in assessed taxes, penalties, and interest, or\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\n$50,000 or less in assessed taxes, penalties, and interest for an out-of-business sole proprietorship\r\n\r\nWithout trust fund taxes [ https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/trust-fund-taxes ]:\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\n$50,000 or less in assessed taxes, penalties, and interest\r\n\r\n*Individuals*\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\n$50,000 or less in assessed taxes, penalties, and interest\r\n\r\nIf a client does not qualify for a Simple Payment Plan, they may still qualify for another type of payment plan [ https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc202 ].\r\n\r\n*How to Apply*\r\n\r\nTo apply for a Simple Payment Plan, businesses or their representatives can call the IRS at 800-829-4933 [ tel:800-829-4933 ] or visit their local Taxpayer Assistance Center (TAC) [ https://apps.irs.gov/app/office-locator/ ]. Individuals can still sign in to their IRS online account [ https://sa.www4.irs.gov/ola ] or call the IRS at the number on their notice or 800-829-1040 [ tel:800-829-1040 ].\r\n\r\nFor more information about Simple Payment Plans, including payment terms and methods of payment, see the Simple Payment Plans for individuals and businesses [ https://www.irs.gov/payments/simple-payment-plans-for-individuals-and-businesses ] page on IRS.gov.\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*5.\xc2\xa0 National Taxpayer Advocate delivers Annual Report to Congress*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nNational Taxpayer Advocate\xe2\x80\x99s 2025 Annual Report to Congress [ https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/AnnualReport2025 ] is now available.\r\n\r\nNational Taxpayer Advocate Erin M. Collins released [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/national-taxpayer-advocate-delivers-annual-report-to-congress-finds-taxpayer-service-was-strong-in-2025-but-foresees-challenges-for-taxpayers-who-encounter-problems-in-2026 ] her 2025 Annual Report to Congress [ https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/AnnualReport2025 ] on January 28. The report acknowledges improvements in IRS taxpayer service but also highlights upcoming challenges, particularly with delays in processing amended returns and confusion surrounding the phasing out of paper refund checks.\r\n\r\nThe Advocate\xe2\x80\x99s report identifies the ten most serious problems taxpayers are experiencing in their dealings with the IRS and makes administrative and legislative recommendations to address those problems. Some key points include recommending credentials for paid tax return preparers, improvements to the Tax Pro Account, and ways IRS can help stop the spread of tax misinformation online.\r\n\r\nPlease visit TaxpayerAdvocate.IRS.gov/AnnualReport2025 [ https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/AnnualReport2025 ] for more information.\r\n\r\nSubscribe [ https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/contact-us/subscribe-to-tas/ ] to receive the National Taxpayer Advocate\xe2\x80\x99s blogs about key issues in tax administration throughout the year or visit TaxpayerAdvocate.IRS.gov [ https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/ ] to read her previous blogs [ https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/taxnews-information/blogs-nta/ ].\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*6.\xc2\xa0 2025 Nationwide Tax Forum Online: How to help taxpayers avoid abusive tax promotions and abusive return preparers*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nOne highlighted session from the IRS Nationwide Tax Forum Online (NTFO) [ https://irstaxforumonline.com/ ] is How to Help Taxpayers Avoid Abusive Tax Promotions and Abusive Return Preparers [ https://irstaxforumonline.com/courses/14730 ]. In this session, the IRS Offices of Fraud Enforcement and Promoter Investigations discuss the IRS\xe2\x80\x99s efforts to address fraud schemes, abusive tax promotions and abusive return preparers. Participants will learn how to protect clients from fraudulent schemes, abusive promotions and return preparers, as well as how to report these to the IRS.\r\n\r\nA previous issue of e-News for Tax Professionals mentioned the session titled How to Avoid Processing Delays and Streamline Return Filing [ https://irstaxforumonline.com/courses/14735 ], but did not include the link to the session.\r\n\r\nAll NTFO self-study seminars cost $29 each. Tax pros can earn one continuing education credit for each NTFO self-study seminar or audit a presentation for free. For more information, visit IrsTaxForumOnline.com [ https://irstaxforumonline.com/ ].\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*7.\xc2\xa0 Reminder: VDP comment period*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThe IRS is seeking input from tax professionals proposed updates to the Voluntary Disclosure Practice (VDP) [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-seeks-public-comment-on-voluntary-disclosure-practice-proposal ].\r\n\r\nThe IRS is moving toward a more efficient, streamlined Voluntary Disclosure Practice. The goal is to make the process easier for taxpayers and their representatives to navigate and more efficient for the agency to administer. The redesigned framework focuses on clarity, predictability, and speed.\r\n\r\nThe public comment period ends March 22, 2026. Tax professionals and others may submit comments by email to vdp@ci.irs.gov, with the subject line \xe2\x80\x9cPROPOSED VDP PUBLIC COMMENT.\xe2\x80\x9d\r\n\r\nIf finalized, the procedures will take effect after the IRS reviews the public comments.\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*8.\xc2\xa0 Did you know? IRS tax tips*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nIRS tax tips [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-tax-tips ] are easy-to-read tips about taxes the IRS sends via e-mail. Tax tips are brief, to the point and cover a wide range of topics.\r\n\r\nTax professionals might be particularly interested in three recent tax tips:\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nReminders for taxpayers about digital assets [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/reminders-for-taxpayers-about-digital-assets ]\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nTax Pros: Become an authorized e-file provider in three steps [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/tax-pros-become-an-authorized-e-file-provider-in-three-steps ]\r\n\r\n\r\n  * \r\n\r\nEnhanced tax incentives for Qualified Opportunity Zone investments in rural areas [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/enhanced-tax-incentives-for-qualified-opportunity-zone-investments-in-rural-areas ]\r\n\r\nTax professionals and their clients can subscribe [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/subscribe-to-irs-tax-tips ] to receive future tax tips from the IRS.\r\n\r\nA white electric fan blowing attached green and pink ribbons. Text: 'Becoming an authorized e-file provider is a breeze.' IRS logo also displayed.\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n*9.\xc2\xa0 Technical guidance*\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThe IRS recently issued guidance on group exemption letter requests and the extension of the SECURE 2.0 Act amendment deadline for IRAs.\r\n\r\n*IRS will accept group exemption letter requests*\r\n\r\nRevenue Procedure 2026-08 [ https://www.irs.gov/irb/2026-04_IRB#REV-PROC-2026-8 ] sets forth procedures for group exemption letter requests for organizations described in section 501(c) of the Internal Revenue Code. Group exemption letter requests are for organizations affiliated with and under the general supervision or control of a central organization. The revenue procedure relieves each subordinate organization included in a group exemption letter from filing its own application for recognition of exemption. It also sets forth updated procedures a central organization must follow to maintain a group exemption letter.\r\n\r\nNotice 2026-08 [ https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/n-26-08.pdf ] discusses the comments received in response to the proposed revenue procedure regarding the group exemption letter program set forth in Notice 2020-36, 2020-21 I.R.B. 840, along with the modifications made in response to those comments and other significant revisions made to the proposed revenue procedure.\r\n\r\nGroup exemption letter requests may now be submitted using Form 8940, Request for Miscellaneous Determination [ https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8940 ], through Pay.gov [ https://www.pay.gov/public/home ]. The required user fee for requesting a group exemption letter is currently $3,500.\r\n\r\nRevenue Procedure 2026-08 and Notice 2026-08 were in Internal Revenue Bulletin 2026-04 [ https://www.irs.gov/irb/2026-04_IRB ], dated Jan. 20, 2026.\r\n\r\n*Extension of SECURE 2.0 Act Amendment Deadline for IRAs*\r\n\r\nNotice 2026-09 [ https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/n-26-09.pdf ] provides guidance relating to amendments under section 501 of the SECURE 2.0 Act of 2022 for an individual retirement arrangements and annuities (IRAs) under section 408(a), (b), or (h), an employer\xe2\x80\x99s SEP arrangement under section 408(k), and an employer\xe2\x80\x99s SIMPLE IRA plan under section 408(p). This notice provides that the Treasury Department and the IRS have extended the deadline to make certain amendments for IRAs, SEP arrangements and SIMPLE IRA plans to December 31, 2027.\r\n\r\nNotice 2026-09 will be in Internal Revenue Bulletin 2026-07, dated Feb. 9, 2026.\r\n\r\nBack to top [ #top ]\r\n\r\n________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0 \xc2\xa0FaceBook Logo [ https://www.facebook.com/IRS/ ]\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0YouTube Logo [ https://www.youtube.com/user/irsvideos ] \xc2\xa0Instagram Logo [ https://www.instagram.com/irsnews ]\xc2\xa0 Twitter Logo [ https://twitter.com/IRSnews ] \xc2\xa0LinkedIn Logo [ https://www.linkedin.com/company/irs/ ]________________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\nThank you for subscribing to e-News for Tax Professionals an IRS e-mail service.\r\n\r\nIf you have a specific concern about your client's tax situation, call the IRS Practitioner Priority Service 1-866-860-4259.\r\n\r\nThis message was distributed automatically from the mailing list e-News for Tax Professionals. *Please Do Not Reply To This Message *\r\n\r\nTo subscribe to or unsubscribe from another list, please go to the e-News Subscriptions [ https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/e-news-subscriptions ] page on the IRS Web site.\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n\r\nUseful Links:\r\n\r\nIRS.gov [ https://www.irs.gov/ ]\r\n\r\nTax Professionals Home [ https://www.irs.gov/tax-professionals ]\r\n\r\nAll Forms and Instructions [ https://www.irs.gov/forms-instructions ]\r\n\r\nStakeholders Partners'  [ https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/partners-and-stakeholders ]\r\nHeadliners [ https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/partners-and-stakeholders ]\r\n\r\nTraining and [ https://www.irs.gov/tax-professionals/